1
00:00:00,182 --> 00:00:03,698
The right SAT study plan
depends on one thing:

2
00:00:03,718 --> 00:00:05,234
how much time you have.

3
00:00:05,254 --> 00:00:08,790
Two weeks, one month, and three
months need different plans.

4
00:00:08,878 --> 00:00:10,964
But the mistake is the same in all three.

5
00:00:10,984 --> 00:00:13,858
Students take practice
test after practice test,

6
00:00:13,878 --> 00:00:16,786
check the score, and
never fix what went wrong.

7
00:00:16,806 --> 00:00:20,441
Or they study random topics
in no particular order.

8
00:00:20,461 --> 00:00:23,584
In this video, you'll get a
concrete plan for each timeline,

9
00:00:23,604 --> 00:00:26,405
built only on free, official resources.

10
00:00:26,425 --> 00:00:30,179
And if you're short on time, you'll
know exactly what to study first.

11
00:00:30,199 --> 00:00:32,429
Every plan uses the same four tools.

12
00:00:32,449 --> 00:00:35,679
First, the full-length practice
tests in the Bluebook app.

13
00:00:35,699 --> 00:00:39,607
They're free, and College Board says
they use the same adaptive model as the

14
00:00:39,627 --> 00:00:40,627
real test.

15
00:00:40,647 --> 00:00:44,948
Right now, College Board lists eight
full-length SAT practice tests,

16
00:00:44,968 --> 00:00:46,662
numbered four through eleven.

17
00:00:46,682 --> 00:00:50,519
The lineup can change, so check the
Practice and Prepare section in Bluebook for

18
00:00:50,539 --> 00:00:51,520
the current list.

19
00:00:51,540 --> 00:00:54,779
Either way, eight isn't a lot,
so plan when you use them.

20
00:00:54,799 --> 00:00:58,206
After each test, your results
show up in My Practice.

21
00:00:58,226 --> 00:01:00,850
You can see every question, your answer,

22
00:01:00,870 --> 00:01:04,314
the correct answer, and a
breakdown of every answer choice,

23
00:01:04,334 --> 00:01:07,083
plus how you did in each content domain.

24
00:01:07,138 --> 00:01:09,868
Second, the SAT Suite Question Bank.

25
00:01:09,888 --> 00:01:12,440
It has thousands of official questions,

26
00:01:12,460 --> 00:01:15,333
and you can filter by section, domain,

27
00:01:15,353 --> 00:01:16,939
skill, and difficulty.

28
00:01:16,959 --> 00:01:19,797
You can export a set without answers,

29
00:01:19,817 --> 00:01:24,496
do it like a quiz, then export it
again with answers and explanations.

30
00:01:24,577 --> 00:01:28,379
Third, Official SAT Prep on Khan Academy.

31
00:01:28,399 --> 00:01:31,486
It's free, it's the prep
course College Board links to,

32
00:01:31,506 --> 00:01:33,985
and it covers every skill at three levels:

33
00:01:34,005 --> 00:01:36,756
foundations, medium, and advanced.

34
00:01:36,814 --> 00:01:40,722
It also has a walk-through course
for each official practice test,

35
00:01:40,742 --> 00:01:44,135
so you can see an explanation
for every question you took.

36
00:01:44,203 --> 00:01:46,397
And fourth, an error log.

37
00:01:46,417 --> 00:01:48,897
That's a list of every question you miss,

38
00:01:48,917 --> 00:01:51,988
why you missed it, and
the rule that fixes it.

39
00:01:52,131 --> 00:01:55,096
It's the tool that turns
practice into points.

40
00:01:55,167 --> 00:01:58,145
The next video shows
exactly how to build one.

41
00:01:58,165 --> 00:02:01,537
If you're short on time, don't
study everything equally.

42
00:02:01,557 --> 00:02:04,502
Study what shows up most,
where you're weakest.

43
00:02:04,522 --> 00:02:09,001
College Board itself recommends focusing
on the domains that appear most often

44
00:02:09,021 --> 00:02:10,092
on the test.

45
00:02:10,117 --> 00:02:11,724
In math, it's clear.

46
00:02:11,760 --> 00:02:14,883
Algebra is about thirty-five
percent of the section,

47
00:02:14,903 --> 00:02:17,919
and Advanced Math is
another thirty-five percent.

48
00:02:17,939 --> 00:02:21,526
Together, that's about seventy
percent of the math questions.

49
00:02:21,546 --> 00:02:25,419
Problem solving and data
analysis, and geometry and trig,

50
00:02:25,439 --> 00:02:27,617
are about fifteen percent each.

51
00:02:27,651 --> 00:02:30,258
In Reading and Writing, the
four domains are closer.

52
00:02:30,365 --> 00:02:33,401
Craft and Structure is
about twenty-eight percent.

53
00:02:33,579 --> 00:02:36,630
Information and Ideas, and
Standard English Conventions,

54
00:02:36,650 --> 00:02:38,988
are about twenty-six percent each.

55
00:02:39,008 --> 00:02:41,847
Expression of Ideas is
about twenty percent.

56
00:02:41,867 --> 00:02:44,633
So in Reading and Writing,
add one more filter:

57
00:02:44,653 --> 00:02:46,474
how fast can you fix it?

58
00:02:46,831 --> 00:02:50,239
Grammar is a short list of rules
you can learn in a few days.

59
00:02:50,259 --> 00:02:54,061
Transitions come down to a
handful of logical relationships.

60
00:02:54,081 --> 00:02:55,617
Those are quick wins.

61
00:02:55,724 --> 00:02:59,356
Inference and reading skills
usually take longer to move.

62
00:02:59,376 --> 00:03:01,805
Then use your practice score report.

63
00:03:01,982 --> 00:03:05,070
For each domain, it shows
a bar of up to seven boxes,

64
00:03:05,090 --> 00:03:09,125
filled in based on the difficulty of
the questions you showed you can handle.

65
00:03:09,197 --> 00:03:13,518
A heavily tested domain with few
boxes filled is your first priority.

66
00:03:13,589 --> 00:03:14,069
Try it.

67
00:03:14,089 --> 00:03:15,140
Here's a math report.

68
00:03:15,160 --> 00:03:16,676
Algebra has three boxes.

69
00:03:16,696 --> 00:03:18,283
Advanced Math has five.

70
00:03:18,303 --> 00:03:20,925
Problem solving and data analysis has two.

71
00:03:20,945 --> 00:03:22,962
Geometry and trig has four.

72
00:03:22,982 --> 00:03:24,945
Which domain do you study first?

73
00:03:24,965 --> 00:03:26,215
Pause and try it.

74
00:03:29,431 --> 00:03:30,431
Algebra.

75
00:03:30,645 --> 00:03:33,696
Problem solving and data
analysis has fewer boxes,

76
00:03:33,716 --> 00:03:36,716
but it's only about fifteen
percent of the section.

77
00:03:36,966 --> 00:03:39,196
Algebra is about thirty-five percent,

78
00:03:39,216 --> 00:03:42,267
and three boxes leaves
a lot of room to grow.

79
00:03:42,287 --> 00:03:44,716
Start there, then move to problem solving.

80
00:03:44,770 --> 00:03:47,464
Every plan is built from
the same study session.

81
00:03:47,484 --> 00:03:50,484
It takes about an hour,
and it has three parts.

82
00:03:50,505 --> 00:03:52,362
Part one, learn.

83
00:03:52,504 --> 00:03:53,841
About fifteen minutes.

84
00:03:53,861 --> 00:03:58,198
Watch a Khan Academy lesson or read
through explanations for one skill.

85
00:03:58,218 --> 00:04:00,148
Just one skill per session.

86
00:04:00,182 --> 00:04:02,075
Part two, practice.

87
00:04:02,111 --> 00:04:03,054
About thirty minutes.

88
00:04:03,074 --> 00:04:07,804
Pull ten to fifteen Question Bank
questions on that same skill,

89
00:04:07,824 --> 00:04:10,983
at the difficulty that
matches your score report.

90
00:04:11,003 --> 00:04:13,448
Time yourself at about the real pace:

91
00:04:13,468 --> 00:04:16,126
a little over a minute per
Reading and Writing question,

92
00:04:16,146 --> 00:04:19,285
a little over a minute and
a half per math question.

93
00:04:19,305 --> 00:04:20,912
Part three, log.

94
00:04:21,198 --> 00:04:22,357
About fifteen minutes.

95
00:04:22,377 --> 00:04:26,570
Check your answers, put every miss and
every lucky guess in your error log,

96
00:04:26,590 --> 00:04:30,198
and redo a few old entries
without looking at the answer.

97
00:04:30,225 --> 00:04:32,027
Only have thirty minutes a day?

98
00:04:32,047 --> 00:04:35,919
Cut each part in half: about
seven minutes learning,

99
00:04:35,939 --> 00:04:39,027
fifteen practicing, and eight logging.

100
00:04:39,047 --> 00:04:40,311
But don't skip the log.

101
00:04:40,331 --> 00:04:42,567
That's the part that
makes the practice count.

102
00:04:42,587 --> 00:04:45,622
When a plan says skill
day, this is what it means.

103
00:04:45,694 --> 00:04:46,460
Two weeks.

104
00:04:46,480 --> 00:04:48,102
The goal isn't to learn everything.

105
00:04:48,122 --> 00:04:51,265
It's to fix your biggest leaks
and get comfortable with the test.

106
00:04:51,343 --> 00:04:55,823
Day one, take a full Bluebook
practice test in one sitting,

107
00:04:55,843 --> 00:04:57,859
timed, like the real thing.

108
00:04:57,879 --> 00:05:00,216
Day two, review it in My Practice.

109
00:05:00,236 --> 00:05:05,093
Log every miss, and pick your top
three skills using weight and weakness.

110
00:05:05,120 --> 00:05:07,528
Days three through seven are skill days,

111
00:05:07,548 --> 00:05:09,136
about an hour each.

112
00:05:09,156 --> 00:05:11,441
Rotate through your top three skills.

113
00:05:11,477 --> 00:05:16,583
In math, that's almost always
something in Algebra or Advanced Math.

114
00:05:16,620 --> 00:05:20,156
In Reading and Writing, start
with grammar if it's on your list.

115
00:05:20,178 --> 00:05:23,944
College Board recommends spacing practice
tests at least two weeks apart when

116
00:05:23,964 --> 00:05:25,158
your schedule allows.

117
00:05:25,178 --> 00:05:27,230
With two weeks total, it doesn't.

118
00:05:27,250 --> 00:05:30,122
So take your second full
test around day eight,

119
00:05:30,142 --> 00:05:33,285
when there's still almost a
week to fix what it shows.

120
00:05:33,464 --> 00:05:35,036
Day nine, review it.

121
00:05:35,056 --> 00:05:39,536
Days ten through twelve, skill days
on whatever the second test exposed,

122
00:05:39,556 --> 00:05:42,965
plus one timed pacing
drill from the last video.

123
00:05:42,985 --> 00:05:47,092
Day thirteen, redo your error log
and make sure Bluebook is ready.

124
00:05:47,199 --> 00:05:50,488
Day fourteen, light
review only, then rest.

125
00:05:50,508 --> 00:05:51,508
One month.

126
00:05:51,615 --> 00:05:54,024
Now you have time for two full tests,

127
00:05:54,044 --> 00:05:57,202
two weeks apart, the way
College Board suggests,

128
00:05:57,222 --> 00:05:59,364
and real skill-building in between.

129
00:05:59,385 --> 00:06:02,721
Week one, take practice test
one at the start of the week,

130
00:06:02,741 --> 00:06:05,079
review it, and set up your error log.

131
00:06:05,099 --> 00:06:07,186
Pick three or four priority skills.

132
00:06:07,206 --> 00:06:09,706
Then do skill days, five days a week.

133
00:06:09,780 --> 00:06:12,259
Week two, keep working the same skills,

134
00:06:12,279 --> 00:06:16,030
and move up a difficulty level once
you're getting most questions right.

135
00:06:16,280 --> 00:06:18,745
Add one timed pacing drill.

136
00:06:18,776 --> 00:06:21,935
Week three, take practice test
two at the start of the week.

137
00:06:21,955 --> 00:06:25,863
That's two weeks after the first,
and about two weeks before test day,

138
00:06:25,883 --> 00:06:28,041
which matches College Board's advice.

139
00:06:28,061 --> 00:06:31,633
Review it, update your priorities,
and go back to skill days.

140
00:06:31,665 --> 00:06:34,467
Week four, mix skills
together in timed sets,

141
00:06:34,487 --> 00:06:37,931
because the real test doesn't tell
you which skill is coming next.

142
00:06:37,951 --> 00:06:40,645
Redo every question in your error log.

143
00:06:40,665 --> 00:06:42,594
Keep the last two days light.

144
00:06:42,680 --> 00:06:45,767
One habit for the whole
month: one day each week,

145
00:06:45,787 --> 00:06:48,394
usually the weekend,
goes to your error log.

146
00:06:48,466 --> 00:06:52,859
Redo old misses cold, and count
which mistakes keep coming back.

147
00:06:52,913 --> 00:06:53,913
Three months.

148
00:06:54,091 --> 00:06:56,536
This plan gives you time to learn content,

149
00:06:56,556 --> 00:06:57,770
not just patch it.

150
00:06:57,877 --> 00:07:00,625
Split it into three
phases of four weeks each.

151
00:07:00,645 --> 00:07:02,482
Weeks one through four, build.

152
00:07:02,502 --> 00:07:05,196
Take practice test one in week one.

153
00:07:05,216 --> 00:07:09,767
For an extra skill check, Khan Academy's
Course Challenge is a short test across

154
00:07:09,787 --> 00:07:10,911
every skill.

155
00:07:10,931 --> 00:07:13,518
Then learn content in priority order:

156
00:07:13,538 --> 00:07:16,089
Algebra, Advanced Math, grammar rules,

157
00:07:16,109 --> 00:07:18,288
then the Reading and
Writing question types.

158
00:07:18,308 --> 00:07:20,931
Start Question Bank
practice at easy or medium,

159
00:07:20,951 --> 00:07:22,451
depending on your boxes.

160
00:07:22,522 --> 00:07:25,308
Five or six skill days a week is plenty.

161
00:07:25,348 --> 00:07:27,470
Weeks five through eight, apply.

162
00:07:27,490 --> 00:07:30,348
Take practice tests in
weeks five and seven.

163
00:07:30,419 --> 00:07:33,756
Move most of your Question
Bank work to medium and hard.

164
00:07:33,776 --> 00:07:38,340
Add pacing drills twice a week, and
start a weekly error log review.

165
00:07:38,360 --> 00:07:42,911
If your score stalls in this
phase, don't just add more tests.

166
00:07:42,931 --> 00:07:44,268
Look at your error log.

167
00:07:44,288 --> 00:07:47,518
If most misses are content
gaps, go back to lessons.

168
00:07:47,538 --> 00:07:49,875
If they're misreads or careless errors,

169
00:07:49,895 --> 00:07:52,610
the fix is a habit, not more content.

170
00:07:52,644 --> 00:07:54,838
Weeks nine through twelve, perform.

171
00:07:54,858 --> 00:07:57,731
Take practice tests in
weeks nine and eleven.

172
00:07:57,751 --> 00:08:00,730
Spend more time on hard
questions in your top domains,

173
00:08:00,750 --> 00:08:03,124
timed mixed sets, and your error log.

174
00:08:03,144 --> 00:08:04,929
Keep the final week light.

175
00:08:04,998 --> 00:08:07,300
That's five full tests in three months,

176
00:08:07,320 --> 00:08:09,336
two weeks apart after the build phase.

177
00:08:09,356 --> 00:08:13,906
It also leaves three tests unused, which
matters if you end up taking the SAT a

178
00:08:13,926 --> 00:08:14,995
second time.

179
00:08:15,015 --> 00:08:15,817
Quick recap.

180
00:08:15,837 --> 00:08:18,674
Two weeks: one test, fix
your top three skills,

181
00:08:18,694 --> 00:08:20,817
second test around day eight.

182
00:08:20,837 --> 00:08:23,352
One month: two tests, two weeks apart,

183
00:08:23,372 --> 00:08:24,852
skill days in between.

184
00:08:24,872 --> 00:08:29,781
Three months: build, apply, perform, with
tests every two weeks after the first

185
00:08:29,801 --> 00:08:30,566
month.

186
00:08:30,586 --> 00:08:34,280
In every plan, start with the most
tested domains where you're weakest,

187
00:08:34,300 --> 00:08:35,979
and log every miss.

188
00:08:36,010 --> 00:08:37,633
If this helped, subscribe.

189
00:08:37,653 --> 00:08:40,740
The next video shows how to review
a practice test the right way,

190
00:08:40,760 --> 00:08:43,830
including exactly what
goes in your error log.
